{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791634422915,"sessionID":"ses_eda44bd40ffe4TvSBP4dtmqSoU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_XzCuWvZEh2FpURpo9Zxo3804","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/.opencode/jobs/medical_policies"},"output":"<path>/workspace/.opencode/jobs/medical_policies</path>\n<type>directory</type>\n<entries>\nbrief.md\nimplementation.md\nsession.log\ntasks.md\nverdict.md\n\n(5 entries)\n</entries>","metadata":{"preview":"brief.md\nimplementation.md\nsession.log\ntasks.md\nverdict.md","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"directory","path":"/workspace/.opencode/jobs/medical_policies","entries":["brief.md","implementation.md","session.log","tasks.md","verdict.md"],"offset":1,"totalEntries":5,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/jobs/medical_policies","time":{"start":1791634422874,"end":1791634422901}},"id":"prt_125bbbbff001UeC6rAHc9giuxY","sessionID":"ses_eda44bd40ffe4TvSBP4dtmqSoU","messageID":"msg_125bbb566001Tl7jr6GQdxW0VF"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791634423116,"sessionID":"ses_eda44bd40ffe4TvSBP4dtmqSoU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_02_k3gVjlo2HH3yp6o8ex3F6391","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/agents/developer.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/agents/developer.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: developer\n3: description: Implements tasks from a job's tasks.md. One task at a time, committing as it goes.\n4: tools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob\n5: commit: true\n6: permission:\n7:   edit:\n8:     \"*\": allow\n9:   bash:\n10:     \"*\": allow\n11:     \"git worktree*\": deny\n12:     \"git branch -d*\": deny\n13:     \"git branch -D*\": deny\n14:     \"git branch --delete*\": deny\n15:     \"git branch --move*\": deny\n16:     \"git branch --copy*\": deny\n17:     \"git reset*\": deny\n18:     \"git clean*\": deny\n19:     \"git gc*\": deny\n20:     \"git prune*\": deny\n21:     \"git reflog*\": deny\n22:     \"git push*\": deny\n23:     \"git fetch*\": deny\n24:     \"git pull*\": deny\n25:     \"git checkout*\": deny\n26:     \"git switch*\": deny\n27:     \"git restore*\": deny\n28:     \"git stash*\": deny\n29:     \"git remote*\": deny\n30:     \"git tag -d*\": deny\n31:     \"git update-ref*\": deny\n32: ---\n33: \n34: You are a senior developer working on this project.\n35: \n36: ## Before starting\n37: \n38: 1. Read `brief.md` — understand what the job is about\n39: 2. Read `tasks.md` — understand the full task list\n40: 3. Check which branch you are on: `git branch --show-current` — the mounted workspace is the job's own worktree, always on the job branch, so no `git checkout` is needed\n41: 4. Compare against the `branch:` field in `brief.md`\n42: 5. If they differ, stop and report back — do not implement on the wrong branch\n43: \n44: ## For each task, follow these steps in order\n45: \n46: **Step 1 — Read** the relevant files before touching anything.\n47: \n48: **Step 2 — Implement** exactly what the task requires. Nothing more.\n49: - Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\n50: - Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\n51: - Do not install packages without asking first\n52: - If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\n53: \n54: **Step 3 — Commit** once the task is complete. One commit per task is the\n55: recommended pattern, but the exact message format is not required — the whole\n56: branch is squashed into a single commit at `mg done`, so per-task commit\n57: hygiene is not a review criterion.\n58: ```\n59: git add -A\n60: git commit -m \"short description\"\n61: ```\n62: \n63: A task should be committed before moving to the next one. When you finish,\n64: leave the worktree clean: nothing uncommitted, including files earlier agents\n65: left behind (e.g. the analyst's `tasks.md`).\n66: \n67: **Step 4 — Repeat** for the next task.\n68: \n69: ## After all tasks are done\n70: \n71: Write a summary to `implementation.md` in the job directory:\n72: \n73: ```markdown\n74: ## Summary\n75: \n76: Brief overall description of what was implemented.\n77: \n78: ## Changes\n79: \n80: TASK-1: what was done, which files were changed and why\n81: TASK-2: ...\n82: \n83: ## Known issues / follow-ups\n84: \n85: Anything that came up during implementation that wasn't in scope.\n86: If nothing: write \"none\".\n87: ```\n88: \n89: Then commit `implementation.md`:\n90: ```\n91: git commit -m \"[ID] implementation: add summary\"\n92: ```\n93: \n94: ## Hard rules\n95: \n96: - Do not push\n97: - Do not merge\n98: - Do not touch any other branch\n99: - Do not move to the next task without committing the current one (one commit\n100:   per task is the recommended pattern, exact format not required)\n101: - git is restricted in agent sessions: you can read history and make commits\n102:   (`git add`, `git commit`, `git log`, `git diff`, `git show`, `git status`,\n103:   ...) — everything else (worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, clean,\n104:   push, fetch, pull, checkout, stash, merge, rebase, ...) is refused by the\n105:   session's git shim.\n106: \n107: ## Verifying rendered work\n108: \n109: When a task changes UI (markup, CSS, or components that render), verify the\n110: rendered result with the `shot` tool instead of reasoning from code alone:\n111: \n112: - `shot <url>` — render + measure a URL at 1280×900 (PNG + render report to\n113:   `screenshots/` in the job dir)\n114: - `shot <url> --widths 375,768,1280` — responsive review\n115: - `shot <url> --full-page` — full-height capture\n116: - `shot <url> --describe` — vision-model prose (works where `ZHIPU_API_KEY`\n117:   is in the session env, i.e. zai-profile sessions; other profiles get a\n118:   documented \"no key\" error and fall back to the report's measured facts)\n119: \n120: The render report is the objective record — contrast ratios, overflow,\n121: alignment, spacing, font status. Commit the PNG + report with the task when\n122: they're useful for review; prune unhelpful renders before the final summary\n123: commit (screenshot hygiene). Read-only agents never run `shot`; they consume\n124: your artifacts.\n125: \n126: Self-selection rule: if your model cannot see the PNG (many text-only models\n127: cannot), run `shot <url> --describe` and reason from the prose — the vision\n128: layer turns the screenshot into design-relevant text. Correct in both cases:\n129: models that can see images get the PNG directly, models that cannot get prose.\n\n(End of file - total 129 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nname: developer\ndescription: Implements tasks from a job's tasks.md. One task at a time, committing as it goes.\ntools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob\ncommit: true\npermission:\n  edit:\n    \"*\": allow\n  bash:\n    \"*\": allow\n    \"git worktree*\": deny\n    \"git branch -d*\": deny\n    \"git branch -D*\": deny\n    \"git branch --delete*\": deny\n    \"git branch --move*\": deny\n    \"git branch --copy*\": deny\n    \"git reset*\": deny\n    \"git clean*\": deny\n    \"git gc*\": deny\n    \"git prune*\": deny","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/agents/developer.md","text":"---\nname: developer\ndescription: Implements tasks from a job's tasks.md. One task at a time, committing as it goes.\ntools: Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob\ncommit: true\npermission:\n  edit:\n    \"*\": allow\n  bash:\n    \"*\": allow\n    \"git worktree*\": deny\n    \"git branch -d*\": deny\n    \"git branch -D*\": deny\n    \"git branch --delete*\": deny\n    \"git branch --move*\": deny\n    \"git branch --copy*\": deny\n    \"git reset*\": deny\n    \"git clean*\": deny\n    \"git gc*\": deny\n    \"git prune*\": deny\n    \"git reflog*\": deny\n    \"git push*\": deny\n    \"git fetch*\": deny\n    \"git pull*\": deny\n    \"git checkout*\": deny\n    \"git switch*\": deny\n    \"git restore*\": deny\n    \"git stash*\": deny\n    \"git remote*\": deny\n    \"git tag -d*\": deny\n    \"git update-ref*\": deny\n---\n\nYou are a senior developer working on this project.\n\n## Before starting\n\n1. Read `brief.md` — understand what the job is about\n2. Read `tasks.md` — understand the full task list\n3. Check which branch you are on: `git branch --show-current` — the mounted workspace is the job's own worktree, always on the job branch, so no `git checkout` is needed\n4. Compare against the `branch:` field in `brief.md`\n5. If they differ, stop and report back — do not implement on the wrong branch\n\n## For each task, follow these steps in order\n\n**Step 1 — Read** the relevant files before touching anything.\n\n**Step 2 — Implement** exactly what the task requires. Nothing more.\n- Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\n- Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\n- Do not install packages without asking first\n- If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\n\n**Step 3 — Commit** once the task is complete. One commit per task is the\nrecommended pattern, but the exact message format is not required — the whole\nbranch is squashed into a single commit at `mg done`, so per-task commit\nhygiene is not a review criterion.\n```\ngit add -A\ngit commit -m \"short description\"\n```\n\nA task should be committed before moving to the next one. When you finish,\nleave the worktree clean: nothing uncommitted, including files earlier agents\nleft behind (e.g. the analyst's `tasks.md`).\n\n**Step 4 — Repeat** for the next task.\n\n## After all tasks are done\n\nWrite a summary to `implementation.md` in the job directory:\n\n```markdown\n## Summary\n\nBrief overall description of what was implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\nTASK-1: what was done, which files were changed and why\nTASK-2: ...\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\nAnything that came up during implementation that wasn't in scope.\nIf nothing: write \"none\".\n```\n\nThen commit `implementation.md`:\n```\ngit commit -m \"[ID] implementation: add summary\"\n```\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- Do not push\n- Do not merge\n- Do not touch any other branch\n- Do not move to the next task without committing the current one (one commit\n  per task is the recommended pattern, exact format not required)\n- git is restricted in agent sessions: you can read history and make commits\n  (`git add`, `git commit`, `git log`, `git diff`, `git show`, `git status`,\n  ...) — everything else (worktree management, branch -d/-D, reset, clean,\n  push, fetch, pull, checkout, stash, merge, rebase, ...) is refused by the\n  session's git shim.\n\n## Verifying rendered work\n\nWhen a task changes UI (markup, CSS, or components that render), verify the\nrendered result with the `shot` tool instead of reasoning from code alone:\n\n- `shot <url>` — render + measure a URL at 1280×900 (PNG + render report to\n  `screenshots/` in the job dir)\n- `shot <url> --widths 375,768,1280` — responsive review\n- `shot <url> --full-page` — full-height capture\n- `shot <url> --describe` — vision-model prose (works where `ZHIPU_API_KEY`\n  is in the session env, i.e. zai-profile sessions; other profiles get a\n  documented \"no key\" error and fall back to the report's measured facts)\n\nThe render report is the objective record — contrast ratios, overflow,\nalignment, spacing, font status. Commit the PNG + report with the task when\nthey're useful for review; prune unhelpful renders before the final summary\ncommit (screenshot hygiene). Read-only agents never run `shot`; they consume\nyour artifacts.\n\nSelf-selection rule: if your model cannot see the PNG (many text-only models\ncannot), run `shot <url> --describe` and reason from the prose — the vision\nlayer turns the screenshot into design-relevant text. Correct in both cases:\nmodels that can see images get the PNG directly, models that cannot get prose.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":129,"totalLines":129,"truncated":false}},"title":"agents/developer.md","time":{"start":1791634423085,"end":1791634423111}},"id":"prt_125bbbcdf0013OO0dhT1njCVE3","sessionID":"ses_eda44bd40ffe4TvSBP4dtmqSoU","messageID":"msg_125bbb566001Tl7jr6GQdxW0VF"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791634423169,"sessionID":"ses_eda44bd40ffe4TvSBP4dtmqSoU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_3Xzk2MNyJ9wnofcbIAXb6497","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/skills/job-brief/SKILL.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/skills/job-brief/SKILL.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: name: job-brief\n3: description: Use when writing or filling in a job's brief.md for the manigot job workflow. Draft a clear What/Why so the analyst and developer can work from it without guessing.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Writing a job brief\n7: \n8: A `brief.md` is the single source of intent for a job. `@analyst` turns it into\n9: tasks, `@developer` implements those tasks, `@reviewer` checks the result\n10: against it — so a vague brief wastes the whole chain.\n11: \n12: ## Structure\n13: \n14: Write only the body of the `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/brief.md` template, starting\n15: at `## What`:\n16: \n17: - **## What**: what this job changes and how. Concrete, one paragraph plus a bullet list of the notable changes.\n18: - **## Why**: the problem it solves for the user. If you cannot write this in one sentence, you do not understand the job yet.\n19: - **## Out of scope**: what is explicitly NOT in this job, so the developer does not gold-plate.\n20: - **## Notes**: anything the analyst or developer should know before starting.\n21: \n22: Never write the frontmatter (`type`, `id`, `branch`, `date`, `author`). It is\n23: generated host-side by `mg job` when the job is scaffolded — job creation is\n24: a host-only operation (the git worktree/branch it requires is outside what an\n25: agent session can do), never something this skill or an agent performs. This\n26: holds whether you're filling in an already-scaffolded `brief.md` (the\n27: frontmatter is already there — leave it alone) or drafting a brief to hand\n28: back for someone to paste into a not-yet-created job.\n29: \n30: ## Rules\n31: \n32: - State intent, not implementation — leave the how to `@developer`.\n33: - If scope is ambiguous, say so in the brief rather than leaving it implicit.\n34: - Keep it short: the analyst reads it first, and a 500-word brief is fine; a 2000-word one is not.\n\n(End of file - total 34 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\nname: job-brief\ndescription: Use when writing or filling in a job's brief.md for the manigot job workflow. Draft a clear What/Why so the analyst and developer can work from it without guessing.\n---\n\n# Writing a job brief\n\nA `brief.md` is the single source of intent for a job. `@analyst` turns it into\ntasks, `@developer` implements those tasks, `@reviewer` checks the result\nagainst it — so a vague brief wastes the whole chain.\n\n## Structure\n\nWrite only the body of the `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/brief.md` template, starting\nat `## What`:\n\n- **## What**: what this job changes and how. Concrete, one paragraph plus a bullet list of the notable changes.\n- **## Why**: the problem it solves for the user. If you cannot write this in one sentence, you do not understand the job yet.\n- **## Out of scope**: what is explicitly NOT in this job, so the developer does not gold-plate.\n- **## Notes**: anything the analyst or developer should know before starting.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/skills/job-brief/SKILL.md","text":"---\nname: job-brief\ndescription: Use when writing or filling in a job's brief.md for the manigot job workflow. Draft a clear What/Why so the analyst and developer can work from it without guessing.\n---\n\n# Writing a job brief\n\nA `brief.md` is the single source of intent for a job. `@analyst` turns it into\ntasks, `@developer` implements those tasks, `@reviewer` checks the result\nagainst it — so a vague brief wastes the whole chain.\n\n## Structure\n\nWrite only the body of the `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/brief.md` template, starting\nat `## What`:\n\n- **## What**: what this job changes and how. Concrete, one paragraph plus a bullet list of the notable changes.\n- **## Why**: the problem it solves for the user. If you cannot write this in one sentence, you do not understand the job yet.\n- **## Out of scope**: what is explicitly NOT in this job, so the developer does not gold-plate.\n- **## Notes**: anything the analyst or developer should know before starting.\n\nNever write the frontmatter (`type`, `id`, `branch`, `date`, `author`). It is\ngenerated host-side by `mg job` when the job is scaffolded — job creation is\na host-only operation (the git worktree/branch it requires is outside what an\nagent session can do), never something this skill or an agent performs. This\nholds whether you're filling in an already-scaffolded `brief.md` (the\nfrontmatter is already there — leave it alone) or drafting a brief to hand\nback for someone to paste into a not-yet-created job.\n\n## Rules\n\n- State intent, not implementation — leave the how to `@developer`.\n- If scope is ambiguous, say so in the brief rather than leaving it implicit.\n- Keep it short: the analyst reads it first, and a 500-word brief is fine; a 2000-word one is not.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":34,"totalLines":34,"truncated":false}},"title":"skills/job-brief/SKILL.md","time":{"start":1791634422997,"end":1791634423154}},"id":"prt_125bbbc5d0011q6ZHeGnjwuTBX","sessionID":"ses_eda44bd40ffe4TvSBP4dtmqSoU","messageID":"msg_125bbb566001Tl7jr6GQdxW0VF"}}
