{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791634517152,"sessionID":"ses_eda43d32bffehZumb2jEv7Tiam","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_00_uRziSFVKnixnoOQmTsrT5400","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && echo \"=== secrets ===\"; grep -rin -e secret -e credential -e token -e api.key -e hardcod -e password agents prompts skills 2>/dev/null; echo \"=== debug/leftover ===\"; grep -rin -e debug -e \"commented-out\" -e \"dead code\" -e TODO -e leftover agents prompts skills 2>/dev/null; echo \"=== smallest/scope ===\"; grep -rin -e \"smallest\" -e \"scope\" -e \"adjacent\" -e refactor agents prompts skills 2>/dev/null"},"output":"=== secrets ===\nagents/sysadmin.md:10:You are hands-on: you inspect config, run commands, and change what's broken. You work over SSH against the server(s) the request names, from the credentials and access available to you. You never guess your way through an operation you cannot verify — if you lack access or a command's effect is unclear, you say so.\nagents/sysadmin.md:62:- If a change requires credentials, root, or access you don't have, stop and report rather than guessing\nagents/architect.md:42:- Operational baseline: backups, monitoring, logging, secrets management, scaling\nagents/architect.md:70:**Hosting and deployment:** Where it runs, how it gets there, and the operational baseline (monitoring, backups, secrets).\nagents/developer.md:116:- `shot <url> --describe` — vision-model prose (works where `ZHIPU_API_KEY`\nagents/designer.md:76:2. Identify the existing design tokens — CSS variables, Tailwind config, or whatever system is in use\nagents/designer.md:96:**Tailwind:** Prefer existing scale values (space-4, text-lg) over arbitrary values ([17px]). If arbitrary values are appearing frequently, it's a sign the config needs custom tokens.\nagents/designer.md:98:**Svelte:** Scoped styles are fine for component-specific overrides. Global design tokens belong in app.css or a dedicated tokens file, not scattered across components.\nagents/devops.md:21:- Secrets and environment configuration in pipelines\nagents/devops.md:62:- If a change requires credentials or access you don't have, stop and report rather than guessing\nagents/prompter.md:45:- Length guidance only when it matters (\"one paragraph\", \"under 200 tokens\")\nagents/security.md:70:- Hardcoded credentials or tokens in source (not in .env — those are already scrubbed)\nagents/security.md:87:- Weak hashing algorithms (MD5, SHA1 for passwords)\nagents/security.md:88:- Hardcoded secrets, weak keys, or predictable tokens\nagents/security.md:106:- API tokens or secrets in frontend bundle\nskills/frontend-design/SKILL.md:32:Work in two passes. First, brainstorm a short design plan based on the human's design brief: create a compact token system with color, type, layout, and signature. Color: describe the palette as 4–6 named hex values. Type: the typefaces for 2+ roles (a characterful display face that's used with restraint, a complementary body face, and a utility face for captions or data if needed). Layout: a layout concept, using one-sentence prose descriptions and ASCII wireframes to ideate and compare. Signature: the single unique element this page will be remembered by that embodies the brief in an appropriate way.\nskills/frontend-design/SKILL.md:42:Spend your boldness in one place. Let the signature element be the one memorable thing, keep everything around it quiet and disciplined, and cut any decoration that does not serve the brief. Not taking a risk can be a risk itself! Build to a quality floor without announcing it: responsive down to mobile, visible keyboard focus, reduced motion respected. Critique your own work as you build, taking screenshots if your environment supports it – a picture is worth 1000 tokens. Consider Chanel's advice: before leaving the house, take a look in the mirror and remove one accessory. Human creators have memory and always try to do something new, so if you have a space to quickly jot down notes about what you've tried, it can help you in future passes.\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:90:   echo \"=== Secrets available in workflow: ===\"\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:106:   **This reveals:** Which layer fails (secrets → workflow ✓, workflow → build ✗)\n=== debug/leftover ===\nagents/quality.md:92:- General: no dead code, no commented-out code, no debug output, no TODO comments left without a tracking issue\nagents/devops.md:3:description: Expert for pipelines and getting things running — CI/CD, builds, deployment, infrastructure, and getting services up locally or remotely. Use when setting up or debugging builds, deployments, containers, or any environment that won't just run.\nagents/security.md:76:- Stack traces or debug output enabled in non-development environments\nagents/security.md:78:- Unprotected admin or debug routes\nskills/webapp-testing/SKILL.md:3:description: Toolkit for interacting with and testing local web applications using Playwright. Supports verifying frontend functionality, debugging UI behavior, capturing browser screenshots, and viewing browser logs.\nskills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md:224:| \"TDD will slow me down\" | TDD IS the pragmatic path: catches bugs before commit, prevents regressions, lets you refactor without fear. \"Pragmatic\" shortcuts mean debugging in production — slower, not faster. |\nskills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md:307:## Debugging Integration\nskills/systematic-debugging/defense-in-depth.md:18:- Debug logging helps when other layers fail\nskills/systematic-debugging/defense-in-depth.md:72:### Layer 4: Debug Instrumentation\nskills/systematic-debugging/defense-in-depth.md:78:  logger.debug('About to git init', {\nskills/systematic-debugging/defense-in-depth.md:93:3. **Add validation at each layer** - Entry, business, environment, debug\nskills/systematic-debugging/defense-in-depth.md:120:- Debug logging identified structural misuse\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:2:name: systematic-debugging\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:6:# Systematic Debugging\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:12:**Violating the letter of this process is violating the spirit of debugging.**\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:249:| \"Emergency, no time for process\" | Systematic debugging is FASTER than guess-and-check thrashing. |\nskills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md:279:These techniques are part of systematic debugging and available in this directory:\nskills/systematic-debugging/condition-based-waiting.md:82:See `condition-based-waiting-example.ts` in this directory for complete implementation with domain-specific helpers (`waitForEvent`, `waitForEventCount`, `waitForEventMatch`) from actual debugging session.\nskills/systematic-debugging/condition-based-waiting.md:111:From debugging session (2025-10-03):\nskills/systematic-debugging/PROVENANCE.md:3:## systematic-debugging\nskills/systematic-debugging/PROVENANCE.md:6:  `skills/systematic-debugging/` (fetched at commit\nskills/systematic-debugging/condition-based-waiting-example.ts:138:// Usage example from actual debugging session:\nskills/systematic-debugging/root-cause-tracing.md:74:  console.error('DEBUG git init:', {\nskills/systematic-debugging/root-cause-tracing.md:89:npm test 2>&1 | grep 'DEBUG git init'\nskills/systematic-debugging/root-cause-tracing.md:165:From debugging session (2025-10-03):\n=== smallest/scope ===\nagents/sysadmin.md:54:4. Make the smallest change that fixes it, stating the impact on the running service\nagents/owner.md:3:description: Reviews features and decisions from the product and user perspective. Use when planning new features, evaluating scope, or sanity-checking that what's being built actually serves the end user.\nagents/owner.md:40:**Scope**\nagents/owner.md:42:- What's explicitly out of scope and should stay that way?\nagents/owner.md:73:- SHIP — this serves the user, fits the product, scope is clear, build it\nagents/owner.md:81:**Scope assessment:** Is the request clear enough to act on? What's missing or ambiguous?\nagents/developer.md:49:- Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\nagents/developer.md:50:- Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\nagents/developer.md:52:- If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\nagents/developer.md:85:Anything that came up during implementation that wasn't in scope.\nagents/designer.md:68:- Do not suggest design changes that require significant structural refactoring unless they're genuinely necessary\nagents/designer.md:98:**Svelte:** Scoped styles are fine for component-specific overrides. Global design tokens belong in app.css or a dedicated tokens file, not scattered across components.\nagents/devops.md:54:4. Make the smallest change that fixes it\nagents/devops.md:61:- Make only the changes the request calls for — no incidental refactoring of unrelated config\nagents/prompter.md:39:- The scope — what's in and what's deliberately out\nagents/reviewer.md:3:description: Reviews changes against the original task requirements. Correctness only — did each task do what was asked, are there bugs, is the scope clean. Read-only. Use after developer has completed all tasks for a job.\nagents/reviewer.md:81:**Scope**\nagents/reviewer.md:83:- Was anything refactored that wasn't part of the task? Flag it.\nagents/security.md:101:- Tenant scope not applied to all queries where it should be\nagents/analyst.md:34:If something is unclear, say so — do not guess at scope.\nprompts/meta.md:10:  changes over broad sweeping ones. When scope is unclear, ask rather than\nskills/job-brief/SKILL.md:19:- **## Out of scope**: what is explicitly NOT in this job, so the developer does not gold-plate.\nskills/job-brief/SKILL.md:33:- If scope is ambiguous, say so in the brief rather than leaving it implicit.\nskills/test-driven-development/SKILL.md:21:- Refactoring\nskills/test-driven-