{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791634522860,"sessionID":"ses_eda43d32bffehZumb2jEv7Tiam","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_yyfqtq2yTMrbQxS5CI3l0600","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/prompts/interactive.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/prompts/interactive.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # manigot — interactive-session addendum\n2: \n3: This layers under the system-wide meta prompt and only reaches interactive\n4: sessions — a human is directly present and able to answer questions, unlike\n5: `--print`/`mg jdi` runs, which never see this file. Your agent file and the\n6: project context remain the operative per-role instructions; this addendum\n7: adjusts how you carry them out when someone is actually here to talk to.\n8: \n9: ## Ask before you sprawl\n10: \n11: - Don't chain a large batch of actions unattended just because you technically\n12:   can. When a request is ambiguous, or the next step is broad, irreversible,\n13:   or a guess about intent, check in rather than running with your best guess.\n14:   A human is right here — use that.\n15: - This does not mean stall on everything. Small, obvious, reversible steps\n16:   don't need a check-in. Save it for the moments where a wrong guess would\n17:   cost real rework.\n18: \n19: ## Talk, don't just deliver a form\n20: \n21: - If your agent instructions describe a structured deliverable — a verdict, a\n22:   fixed set of sections, a scored rubric — that shape is for when you're\n23:   actually asked to review or decide something, or when you're wrapping one\n24:   up. It is not the required shape of every reply.\n25: - When the human is thinking out loud, exploring an idea, or just asking\n26:   questions, have a normal conversation. Don't force the conversation toward\n27:   a formal output it doesn't need yet.\n\n(End of file - total 27 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# manigot — interactive-session addendum\n\nThis layers under the system-wide meta prompt and only reaches interactive\nsessions — a human is directly present and able to answer questions, unlike\n`--print`/`mg jdi` runs, which never see this file. Your agent file and the\nproject context remain the operative per-role instructions; this addendum\nadjusts how you carry them out when someone is actually here to talk to.\n\n## Ask before you sprawl\n\n- Don't chain a large batch of actions unattended just because you technically\n  can. When a request is ambiguous, or the next step is broad, irreversible,\n  or a guess about intent, check in rather than running with your best guess.\n  A human is right here — use that.\n- This does not mean stall on everything. Small, obvious, reversible steps\n  don't need a check-in. Save it for the moments where a wrong guess would\n  cost real rework.\n\n## Talk, don't just deliver a form\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/prompts/interactive.md","text":"# manigot — interactive-session addendum\n\nThis layers under the system-wide meta prompt and only reaches interactive\nsessions — a human is directly present and able to answer questions, unlike\n`--print`/`mg jdi` runs, which never see this file. Your agent file and the\nproject context remain the operative per-role instructions; this addendum\nadjusts how you carry them out when someone is actually here to talk to.\n\n## Ask before you sprawl\n\n- Don't chain a large batch of actions unattended just because you technically\n  can. When a request is ambiguous, or the next step is broad, irreversible,\n  or a guess about intent, check in rather than running with your best guess.\n  A human is right here — use that.\n- This does not mean stall on everything. Small, obvious, reversible steps\n  don't need a check-in. Save it for the moments where a wrong guess would\n  cost real rework.\n\n## Talk, don't just deliver a form\n\n- If your agent instructions describe a structured deliverable — a verdict, a\n  fixed set of sections, a scored rubric — that shape is for when you're\n  actually asked to review or decide something, or when you're wrapping one\n  up. It is not the required shape of every reply.\n- When the human is thinking out loud, exploring an idea, or just asking\n  questions, have a normal conversation. Don't force the conversation toward\n  a formal output it doesn't need yet.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":27,"totalLines":27,"truncated":false}},"title":"prompts/interactive.md","time":{"start":1791634522672,"end":1791634522856}},"id":"prt_125bd419e001IHkQWX4T0vDcjy","sessionID":"ses_eda43d32bffehZumb2jEv7Tiam","messageID":"msg_125bd2e6f001KuCrytHQOJ5UUO"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791634522900,"sessionID":"ses_eda43d32bffehZumb2jEv7Tiam","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01_G8mWkUNTGM7HzRG4iBZe1728","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && sed -n '80,100p' agents/quality.md && echo \"=== developer 45-90 ===\" && sed -n '45,90p' agents/developer.md"},"output":"- Are new functions/classes/components a reasonable size, or are they doing too much?\n- Is responsibility clearly assigned — does each unit have a clear owner?\n\n**Consistency**\n- Does the new code follow the same patterns as the existing codebase?\n- Same error handling style, same naming conventions, same file organisation?\n- If the codebase uses a specific pattern everywhere, new code should follow it — not introduce a different one without reason.\n- Inconsistency is a maintenance burden: future readers have to understand two ways of doing the same thing.\n\n**Modern practices for this stack**\n- PHP: proper typing, return types, no raw queries, no `var_dump` left in, appropriate Laravel conventions (form requests, policies, resources), no static methods where dependency injection fits better\n- JS/Svelte: no `var`, appropriate reactivity patterns, no direct DOM manipulation where Svelte handles it, props typed where possible\n- General: no dead code, no commented-out code, no debug output, no TODO comments left without a tracking issue\n\n**Defensiveness**\n- Are inputs validated where they enter the system?\n- Are error states handled explicitly or silently swallowed?\n- Are edge cases considered — empty arrays, null values, zero, large inputs, concurrent access?\n\n## What you do NOT do\n\n=== developer 45-90 ===\n\n**Step 1 — Read** the relevant files before touching anything.\n\n**Step 2 — Implement** exactly what the task requires. Nothing more.\n- Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\n- Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\n- Do not install packages without asking first\n- If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\n\n**Step 3 — Commit** once the task is complete. One commit per task is the\nrecommended pattern, but the exact message format is not required — the whole\nbranch is squashed into a single commit at `mg done`, so per-task commit\nhygiene is not a review criterion.\n```\ngit add -A\ngit commit -m \"short description\"\n```\n\nA task should be committed before moving to the next one. When you finish,\nleave the worktree clean: nothing uncommitted, including files earlier agents\nleft behind (e.g. the analyst's `tasks.md`).\n\n**Step 4 — Repeat** for the next task.\n\n## After all tasks are done\n\nWrite a summary to `implementation.md` in the job directory:\n\n```markdown\n## Summary\n\nBrief overall description of what was implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\nTASK-1: what was done, which files were changed and why\nTASK-2: ...\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\nAnything that came up during implementation that wasn't in scope.\nIf nothing: write \"none\".\n```\n\nThen commit `implementation.md`:\n```\n","metadata":{"output":"- Are new functions/classes/components a reasonable size, or are they doing too much?\n- Is responsibility clearly assigned — does each unit have a clear owner?\n\n**Consistency**\n- Does the new code follow the same patterns as the existing codebase?\n- Same error handling style, same naming conventions, same file organisation?\n- If the codebase uses a specific pattern everywhere, new code should follow it — not introduce a different one without reason.\n- Inconsistency is a maintenance burden: future readers have to understand two ways of doing the same thing.\n\n**Modern practices for this stack**\n- PHP: proper typing, return types, no raw queries, no `var_dump` left in, appropriate Laravel conventions (form requests, policies, resources), no static methods where dependency injection fits better\n- JS/Svelte: no `var`, appropriate reactivity patterns, no direct DOM manipulation where Svelte handles it, props typed where possible\n- General: no dead code, no commented-out code, no debug output, no TODO comments left without a tracking issue\n\n**Defensiveness**\n- Are inputs validated where they enter the system?\n- Are error states handled explicitly or silently swallowed?\n- Are edge cases considered — empty arrays, null values, zero, large inputs, concurrent access?\n\n## What you do NOT do\n\n=== developer 45-90 ===\n\n**Step 1 — Read** the relevant files before touching anything.\n\n**Step 2 — Implement** exactly what the task requires. Nothing more.\n- Make the smallest change that correctly fulfills the task\n- Do not refactor adjacent code unless the task explicitly requires it\n- Do not install packages without asking first\n- If the task turns out to be more complex than described: stop and report back, do not expand scope\n\n**Step 3 — Commit** once the task is complete. One commit per task is the\nrecommended pattern, but the exact message format is not required — the whole\nbranch is squashed into a single commit at `mg done`, so per-task commit\nhygiene is not a review criterion.\n```\ngit add -A\ngit commit -m \"short description\"\n```\n\nA task should be committed before moving to the next one. When you finish,\nleave the worktree clean: nothing uncommitted, including files earlier agents\nleft behind (e.g. the analyst's `tasks.md`).\n\n**Step 4 — Repeat** for the next task.\n\n## After all tasks are done\n\nWrite a summary to `implementation.md` in the job directory:\n\n```markdown\n## Summary\n\nBrief overall description of what was implemented.\n\n## Changes\n\nTASK-1: what was done, which files were changed and why\nTASK-2: ...\n\n## Known issues / follow-ups\n\nAnything that came up during implementation that wasn't in scope.\nIf nothing: write \"none\".\n```\n\nThen commit `implementation.md`:\n```\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && sed -n '80,100p' agents/quality.md && echo \"=== developer 45-90 ===\" && sed -n '45,90p' agents/developer.md","time":{"start":1791634522768,"end":1791634522867}},"id":"prt_125bd42380012rUFky35inGRhG","sessionID":"ses_eda43d32bffehZumb2jEv7Tiam","messageID":"msg_125bd2e6f001KuCrytHQOJ5UUO"}}
